Subject: ECON: Side note on investment thread...

Peter Baumbach:

>Small companies with heavy debt. That sounds like a good
>idea. Certianly companies with a lot of cash will be hard
>hit.

I'd like to point out that a large company with heavy debt would
be a much better bet, but _only_ if the management has shown that
it is capable of reacting very fast to upsize and downsize the
company to reflect the current market situation. It seems to me
that when a market goes sour, companies don't downsize fast enough.
Although usually when they do, they downsize the wrong things. But
more later.

Of course, if they've shown this responsiveness, they aren't
going to have _heavy_ debt, unless they have a financial advisor
who can predict everything... you know, psychic or Eric Raymond
or something like that. (Or better... the Jupiter-sized brain or
something. Has anyone seen US Sprint's latest commercials for the
"Most" service, w/ the "billion megabyte brain?" What was the first
word that came to mind when you saw it?) 

I think Perry has said that the best investment Americans can make
at the present moment is to get rid of their short-term debt. Think
about that and the decisions the company was making before deceiding
that a _lot_ of debt is a good thing to look for, even in a hyper-
inflationary setup.

In short, of the many things that are good to have done before a
hyperinflationary setup, acquiring a lot of debt _appears_ to be
the one that has the most bad repercussions should said inflation
_not_ take place, and is probably not the best thing to do if it
_does_ take place.

Comments?

Phil

