Date: Mon, 9 Aug 93 22:54:18 -0400
From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
Subject: INVEST: $100 billion daydream

 pmetzger@lehman.com ("Perry E. Metzger") writes:
>If I had 100 Billion in CASH to invest today, I think I could do
>pretty well with it -- there are several projects I can think of that
>I could do if I had resources like that which would produce
>spectacular returns. On the other hand, it is obvious that you can't
>invest 100 Billion straight in normal markets -- you move the market
>too much in so doing.

 I can imagine spending $10-20 billion on asteroid retrieval/space
industrialization and $1 billion on a direct computer-brain link in the
next decade or two, but spending more on those projects would produce
low returns on the additional investment because the supply of good
employees is limited, and increased incentives will be as likely to
enable them to retire (like Tim May) than to convince them to work
harder. Possibly nanotech would be a useful place to make large
investments in new projects - but I don't understand area well enough
to put any numbers on it.
 Most other areas I can think of already have companies doing enough
work on them that buying their stock is likely to be at least as
productive as starting new companies. So I have some trouble believing
that the last $50 billion of that cash could produce above average
returns.

 szabo@netcom.com  (Nick Szabo) writes:
>$5 billion: buy Autodesk or other major CAD company, and expand
>into offering highly automated science & engineering services.
>One goal (initially just charity research) would be the automation 
>of scientific discovery, eg via linking automated labs to genetic
>programming/symbolic regression.  Specially targeted would be
>symbolic regression of brain data and consequent designs for
>implants, drugs, etc.

 Buying a company like Autodesk is not very productive; it already
does a good job of what it does. Completely new projects can be started
more cost-effectively from scratch then by spending money to convince
a successful existing management that their operations can be improved
by radical changes. (I think your notion of automated scientific
invention is a naive dream, but I don't have time to argue about that).

>$15 billion: digital markets & "crypto-anarchy" stuff: electronic
>banking, digital cash, secure phones, decentralized on-line markets,
>programmed trading operations (my own specialty), data havens, etc.

 It is hard for me to imagine how $15 billion can be usefully spent
on things which primarily need standardization (PR for standard X?
bribing governments not to interfere? neither would produce much
profit for the person spending the money). (This is not intended
as a criticism of Nick, who isn't making claims of spectacular
returns on investment; I am pointing out the difficulties of profitably
investing large sums).
 
