From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Natural Law

>From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
>Subject: Disagreement about Natural Law 
>
>starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu says:
>
>> In
>> fact, Perry's position on the nature of rights is most similar to a
>> combination of Hobbesian and Rousseaean ideas.
>
>Thats untrue -- they bear very little resemblance at all.

You've referred to "rights" as "social constructs."  Rousseau originated
social-constructionism.  I call this the "creationist" theory of rights,
as opposed to the "discovery" theory.

Perhaps what you mean is that you stipulated a different meaning to the
term without indicating as much?

>Have *you*
>actually *read* "Leviathan" or "Emile"?

Among other things, although I'm sure I can't quote verbatim, nor do I
possess the books any longer, not having found them worth keeping.  I
recently had my memory refreshed on Hobbes in the chapter Douglas Den Uyl
devotes to him in "The Virtue of Prudence."  Rousseau I'm a bit more
rusty on, but I'll scan through the chapter on him in Paul Johnson's
"Intellectuals" just to check myself.

BTW, those were hardly the only books they wrote.  I've no idea if the
ideas I'm thinking of came from them or from other works of theirs.  It's
all too much a jumble in my mind.

I do know that both Hobbes and Descartes began by opposing almost all of
the Western philosophical tradition that they inherited as false, and
that they ended up repeating many of the mistakes of philosophical
history because they were ignorant of them - this is a trend which
continues through modernity, and is explained by Mortimer Adler in
"Ten Philosophical Mistakes."

